WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.579
Cognitive Load Theory is a practical framework for designing learning around the limits of working memory.

2
00:00:06.579 --> 00:00:16.859
It starts with a simple reality: when people learn something new, they can only hold and process a limited amount of information at one time.

3
00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:30.018
If a lesson asks learners to manage too many new elements, unclear examples, busy visuals, and unfamiliar terms all at once, learning can fail even when the learners are capable and motivated.

4
00:00:30.018 --> 00:00:38.653
At a practical level, working memory is the mental workspace learners use while they are trying to understand something right now.

5
00:00:38.653 --> 00:00:43.999
Long-term memory is where organized knowledge, patterns, procedures, and concepts are stored.

6
00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:51.811
Learning happens when instruction helps people process new information well enough to build useful structures in long-term memory.

7
00:00:51.811 --> 00:00:56.746
Once those structures exist, future tasks become easier to understand and perform.

8
00:00:56.746 --> 00:01:00.036
Cognitive load is not a judgment about intelligence.

9
00:01:00.036 --> 00:01:02.503
It is an instructional design concern.

10
00:01:02.503 --> 00:01:09.082
A confusing course can overwhelm learners with presentation friction, even when the topic itself is reasonable.

11
00:01:09.082 --> 00:01:18.128
A difficult topic can also be taught in a way that manages the load carefully, building foundations before asking for independent performance.

12
00:01:18.128 --> 00:01:24.296
The difference matters because learners often blame themselves when the design is the real problem.

13
00:01:24.296 --> 00:01:27.586
The goal is not to make learning effortless.

14
00:01:27.586 --> 00:01:32.109
Some mental effort is necessary because understanding and skill require work.

15
00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.988
The goal is to make effort productive.

16
00:01:34.988 --> 00:01:42.801
Good design reduces unnecessary burden, sequences complexity, uses examples well, and helps learners focus on the relationships that matter.

17
00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:52.669
Cognitive Load Theory gives designers a language for deciding which difficulty belongs to the task and which difficulty was accidentally created by the lesson.
